      "claim": "The destruction of the Old Bridge of Mostar violated Article 16 of Additional Protocol II because the attack was not justified by military necessity despite the bridge’s occasional use for combat.",
      "reasoning": "Article 16 prohibits any acts of hostility directed against historic monuments unless such acts are used in support of the military effort and are justified by military necessity. The bridge, a sixteenth‑century cultural monument, was indeed used for limited combat activities, but the Trial Chamber found that the HVO’s primary purpose was to sap Muslim morale, not to achieve a decisive military advantage. Consequently, the destruction was not imperatively demanded by military necessity and therefore breached the prohibition of Article 16.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_ii",
          "article": "16",
          "quoted_text": "it is prohibited to commit any acts of hostility directed against historic monuments, works of art or places of worship which constitute the cultural or spiritual heritage of peoples, and to use them in support of the military effort."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The destruction of the Old Bridge of Mostar constituted a war crime under Article 8(2)(a)(iv) of the Rome Statute because it involved extensive destruction of property, was not justified by military necessity, and was carried out wantonly.",
      "reasoning": "Article 8(2)(a)(iv) criminalises \"extensive destruction and appropriation of property, not justified by military necessity and carried out unlawfully and wantonly\". The HVO’s tank attack caused the bridge’s collapse, an act of extensive destruction of a unique cultural property. The Chamber’s findings that the bridge’s loss was intended chiefly to demoralise the civilian population and that no indispensable military advantage resulted demonstrate the absence of military necessity. Moreover, the deliberate targeting of the bridge for psychological effect, without regard to civilian impact, satisfies the \"wanton\" element, which requires that the destruction be carried out with reckless disregard for civilian harm.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(a)(iv)",
          "quoted_text": "Extensive destruction and appropriation of property, not justified by military necessity and carried out unlawfully and wantonly;"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

